WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.747
This is the first runnable PostgreSQL example in the module.

00:00:04.747 --> 00:00:05.732
Do not try to memorize it.

00:00:05.732 --> 00:00:12.107
Read it from top to bottom and identify the purpose of each statement.

00:00:12.107 --> 00:00:17.117
The first statement removes an earlier practice table if it exists so the example is safe to run again.

00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:21.247
CREATE TABLE defines a small table.

00:00:21.247 --> 00:00:21.742
INSERT INTO adds one row.

00:00:21.742 --> 00:00:24.242
SELECT reads the rows back.

00:00:24.242 --> 00:00:28.717
Before you click Run, predict the final output.

00:00:28.717 --> 00:00:32.852
You should expect one row with the patient ID and patient name that were inserted.

00:00:32.852 --> 00:00:36.167
Run the code directly in the browser and inspect the result.

00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:43.942
Then, if you want, copy it into pgAdmin's Query Tool and run the same three-step sequence there.

00:00:43.942 --> 00:00:48.527
The goal is simply to connect CREATE, INSERT, and SELECT with what each one does.

00:00:48.527 --> 00:00:53.927
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:53.927 --> 00:00:58.992
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:58.992 --> 00:01:01.862
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:01.862 --> 00:01:08.497
In a real setting such as an online store separating customers, orders, products, and order lines,

00:01:08.497 --> 00:01:15.272
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:15.272 --> 00:01:17.807
before discussing syntax.

00:01:17.807 --> 00:01:23.505
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
